
<h2>Chapter recap</h2>
<ul>
<li>SciPy is a set of tool drawers on top of NumPy arrays; statsmodels adds regression and time-series models with full inference tables. Import the submodule you need.</li>
<li>Every distribution has <code>pdf/pmf, cdf, sf, ppf, rvs, fit</code>. <code>cdf</code> maps value to probability, <code>ppf</code> maps probability to value.</li>
<li>Pick tests by data type, prefer Welch over Student, use rank tests for skewed data, and always report an effect size and an interval, not only p.</li>
<li>The bootstrap gives intervals for any statistic; permutation tests give p-values without formulas. Seed them.</li>
<li><code>minimize</code> for smooth problems, <code>curve_fit</code> for fitting formulas (give <code>p0</code>), <code>root</code>/<code>brentq</code> for equations, <code>linprog</code>/<code>milp</code> for linear plans (minimises, so negate).</li>
<li><code>trapezoid(y, x)</code> and <code>quad</code> integrate; <code>solve_ivp</code> evolves systems; splines and PCHIP interpolate but should not extrapolate.</li>
<li><code>solve</code> not <code>inv</code>; <code>lstsq</code> for fits; sparse matrices for mostly-zero data (build in COO, compute in CSR); use <code>@</code> for products.</li>
<li><code>rfft</code> plus <code>rfftfreq(n, 1/fs)</code> finds frequencies; <code>sosfiltfilt</code> filters without delay; <code>find_peaks</code> needs prominence or distance.</li>
<li>KDTree for neighbours in low dimensions, <code>cdist</code> for small matrices, <code>linkage</code> and <code>kmeans2</code> for quick clustering (scale the columns first).</li>
<li>statsmodels needs <code>add_constant</code> in the array API; formulas add the intercept. Its numbers can be reproduced with NumPy, which is the best way to understand them.</li>
<li>Verify by recovering a known answer, computing two ways, and plotting.</li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Formula / pattern</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>d.cdf(b) - d.cdf(a)</code></td><td>Probability of landing between a and b</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>d.sf(k - 1)</code></td><td>Discrete "k or more"</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mean +/- t.ppf(0.975, n-1) * sem</code></td><td>95% confidence interval for a mean</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=False)</code></td><td>Welch two-group test</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>n = 2 ((z<sub>1-a/2</sub> + z<sub>power</sub>) / d)<sup>2</sup></code></td><td>Sample size per group for standardised effect d</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bootstrap((x,), stat)</code></td><td>Interval for any statistic</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>curve_fit(f, x, y, p0)</code>, <code>sqrt(diag(pcov))</code></td><td>Parameters and standard errors</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linprog(-c, A_ub, b_ub)</code></td><td>Maximise c.x within limits</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>solve(A, b)</code></td><td>Linear system (never <code>inv(A) @ b</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rfftfreq(n, 1/fs)[argmax(abs(rfft(x)))]</code></td><td>Dominant frequency in Hz</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tree.query(q, k)</code></td><td>k nearest neighbours</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>(X'X)<sup>-1</sup>X'y</code>, se = sqrt(diag(s<sup>2</sup>(X'X)<sup>-1</sup>))</td><td>OLS coefficients and standard errors</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Practice set</h2>
<p>Twenty-five problems, graded from easy to expert. Try each before reading the answer.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.1</span> (easy) What is the probability that a standard normal value is at most 1.96? What is it for a value above 1.96?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>stats.norm.cdf(1.96)</code> is about {{=round(stats.norm.cdf(1.96),4)}}, and the upper tail <code>sf(1.96)</code> is {{=round(stats.norm.sf(1.96),4)}}. They add to 1. The two tails together hold 5%, which is why 1.96 is the 95% interval multiplier.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.2</span> (easy, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
from scipy import stats
print(stats.norm.ppf(0.5, loc=10, scale=3))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> it prints {{LAST}}. The 50th percentile of a symmetric distribution is its mean (and median), so <code>loc</code> is returned; <code>scale</code> does not matter here.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.3</span> (easy) You have 30 waiting times in each of two groups, both strongly right-skewed. Which SciPy test do you choose and why?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>stats.mannwhitneyu</code>, because it compares ranks and does not need normality. A good alternative is to log-transform the times and run Welch's <code>ttest_ind</code> if the logs look normal (check with <code>shapiro</code> or a Q-Q plot). Report an effect size either way.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.4</span> (easy, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
from scipy import optimize
res = optimize.minimize(lambda v: (v[0] - 3) ** 2 + 1, x0=[0.0])
print(round(float(res.x[0]), 3), round(float(res.fun), 3))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. The parabola (x - 3)<sup>2</sup> + 1 has its lowest point at x = 3 with value 1. Note that <code>x0</code> is an array, so <code>res.x</code> is an array and we take element 0.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.5</span> (easy) By hand, what does <code>integrate.trapezoid([0, 2, 4], [0, 1, 2])</code> return?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> two trapezoids, each of width 1. The first has heights 0 and 2: area (0 + 2)/2 = 1. The second has heights 2 and 4: area 3. Total 4, which equals the exact area under y = 2x from 0 to 2 (a straight line has no error): {{=float(integrate.trapezoid([0,2,4],[0,1,2]))}}.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.6</span> (easy, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
from scipy import sparse
A = sparse.csr_array(np.array([[0, 0, 3], [4, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]))
print(A.nnz, A.shape, A.toarray().sum())
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. Only the two non-zero entries (3 and 4) are stored, the shape is 3 by 3 and their sum is 7. A sparse matrix remembers values and positions, not the zeros.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.7</span> (medium, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
print(np.interp([0, 1.5, 9], [1, 2], [10, 20]))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. 1.5 is halfway between 1 and 2, so 15. Outside the known range <code>np.interp</code> does not extrapolate: it clamps to the end values (10 on the left, 20 on the right). That silent clamping can hide a data problem.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.8</span> (medium, find the bug) The code is meant to maximise 3x + 2y with x + y at most 4, x and y at least 0. It reports a profit of zero. Why?</p>
@@PYQ
from scipy import optimize
res = optimize.linprog([3, 2], A_ub=[[1, 1]], b_ub=[4], bounds=[(0, None)] * 2)
print(res.x, res.fun)
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> it prints {{LAST}}. <code>linprog</code> minimises, so the best "minimum" of 3x + 2y is at the origin. Fix: pass <code>c=[-3, -2]</code> and negate the result; the true optimum is x = 4, y = 0 with value 12.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.9</span> (medium, find the bug) A speed log is integrated and gives a negative distance. What went wrong?</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
from scipy import integrate
t = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0]); v = np.array([5.0, 6.0, 7.0, 6.0])
print(integrate.trapezoid(t, v))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> it prints {{LAST}}, a negative number. The signature is <code>trapezoid(y, x)</code>: values first. With the arguments swapped the function integrates time with respect to speed, and because speed falls at the end the small steps count backward. Correct call: <code>trapezoid(v, t)</code> = {{=float(integrate.trapezoid([5.0,6.0,7.0,6.0],[0.0,1.0,2.0,3.0]))}}.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.10</span> (medium) A sample of 25 values has mean 50 and standard deviation 10. Give the 95% t interval for the mean.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> standard error = 10 / sqrt(25) = 2. The t quantile with 24 degrees of freedom is {{=round(stats.t.ppf(0.975,24),3)}}. Interval = 50 plus or minus {{=round(stats.t.ppf(0.975,24)*2,2)}}, so ({{=round(50-stats.t.ppf(0.975,24)*2,2)}}, {{=round(50+stats.t.ppf(0.975,24)*2,2)}}). In code: <code>stats.t.interval(0.95, 24, loc=50, scale=2)</code>. Using 1.96 instead of 2.064 would make the interval too narrow for a sample this small.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.11</span> (medium) A table has page A: 30 converted, 70 not; page B: 50 converted, 50 not. Compute the chi-square statistic by hand.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> totals: converted 80, not 120, each page 100, grand 200. Expected converted per page = 100 * 80 / 200 = 40, not converted = 60. Chi-square = sum of (O - E)<sup>2</sup>/E = 100/40 + 100/60 + 100/40 + 100/60 = 2.5 + 1.667 + 2.5 + 1.667 = 8.333, with 1 degree of freedom. Code check: {{=round(stats.chi2_contingency([[30,70],[50,50]],correction=False)[0],3)}}, p = {{=round(stats.chi2_contingency([[30,70],[50,50]],correction=False)[1],4)}}, so the rates 30% and 50% differ significantly.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.12</span> (medium, find the bug) This call fails or returns nonsense. Why?</p>
@@NORUN
def model(a, k, t):                # parameters first, x last
    return a * np.exp(-k * t)
popt, pcov = optimize.curve_fit(model, t, y)
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>curve_fit</code> always passes the x data as the <em>first</em> argument and the parameters after it. Here it would treat <code>a</code> as x and fit <code>k</code> and <code>t</code> as parameters (and complain about the wrong number of values). Fix the signature to <code>model(t, a, k)</code>.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.13</span> (medium, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
from scipy import stats
d = stats.poisson(2)
print(round(d.cdf(2), 4), round(d.sf(2), 4), round(d.pmf(2), 4))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. <code>cdf(2)</code> is P(0, 1 or 2 events), <code>sf(2)</code> is P(3 or more) and the two add to 1. <code>pmf(2)</code> is just P(exactly 2). Mixing these up is the most common discrete-distribution error.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.14</span> (medium) Write code for a 90% bootstrap interval for the 75th percentile of a sample.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong></p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import stats

x = np.random.default_rng(0).gamma(2.0, 3.0, 80)
r = stats.bootstrap((x,), lambda s, axis: np.percentile(s, 75, axis=axis), n_resamples=3000,
                    confidence_level=0.90, random_state=0)
print("p75 = %.2f, 90%% CI (%.2f, %.2f)" % (np.percentile(x, 75), r.confidence_interval.low, r.confidence_interval.high))
@@END
<ol><li>The data goes in as a tuple <code>(x,)</code>.</li><li>The default is vectorised: the statistic receives a batch of resamples and an <code>axis</code> argument, so the lambda takes <code>axis</code> and passes it to <code>np.percentile</code>. Forgetting <code>axis</code> is the usual cause of errors or wrong shapes here.</li><li><code>confidence_level=0.90</code> gives a 90% interval.</li></ol>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.15</span> (hard, find the bug) A colleague fits a normal to data, then runs <code>stats.kstest(data, "norm", args=(data.mean(), data.std()))</code> and reports "p = 0.4, so the data is normal". Find two problems.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (1) The parameters were estimated from the same data being tested, which makes the KS p-value too large (too forgiving); the proper fix is a Lilliefors-style test or a parametric bootstrap that repeats the estimate on simulated data. (2) A large p-value does not prove normality; it only says the test could not reject it. Complement with a Q-Q plot (<code>stats.probplot</code>) and, with small samples, remember the test has little power.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.16</span> (hard) How many users per group do you need to detect a standardised difference of 0.3 with 80% power at a two-sided 5% level?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> with the normal approximation, n = 2 * ((z<sub>0.975</sub> + z<sub>0.80</sub>) / d)<sup>2</sup> = 2 * ((1.96 + 0.8416) / 0.3)<sup>2</sup> = {{=int(np.ceil(2*((stats.norm.ppf(0.975)+stats.norm.ppf(0.8))/0.3)**2))}} per group (rounded up). Exact t-based calculations (statsmodels <code>TTestIndPower</code>, not run here) give a slightly larger number. Halving the effect size quadruples the sample size, which is why small effects are expensive.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.17</span> (hard) A user-item matrix has 1,000,000 rows, 1,000,000 columns and 5,000,000 non-zero values. Estimate the memory of dense float64 and of CSR with 32-bit indices.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> dense: 10<sup>12</sup> cells * 8 bytes = 8 * 10<sup>12</sup> bytes = 8 TB, impossible. CSR: values 5 * 10<sup>6</sup> * 8 = 40 MB, column indices 5 * 10<sup>6</sup> * 4 = 20 MB, row pointers (10<sup>6</sup> + 1) * 4 = 4 MB, total about 64 MB. Density is 5 * 10<sup>-6</sup>, so sparse is the only option.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.18</span> (hard, what does it print?)</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
from scipy import stats
print(stats.zscore([1, 2, 3]).round(4), stats.zscore([1, 2, 3], ddof=1).round(4))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. By default <code>zscore</code> divides by the population standard deviation (<code>ddof=0</code>: sqrt(2/3) = 0.8165), giving plus or minus 1.2247. With <code>ddof=1</code> the sample standard deviation is 1, giving plus or minus 1. pandas' <code>std</code> uses <code>ddof=1</code> by default, so results can differ between libraries unless you set it.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.19</span> (hard) Find the x where cos(x) = x.</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize
print(round(optimize.brentq(lambda x: np.cos(x) - x, 0, 1), 6))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> {{LAST}}. Define f(x) = cos x - x, which is positive at 0 (value 1) and negative at 1 (about -0.46), so a root lies in [0, 1] and <code>brentq</code> is guaranteed to find it. Check: cos(0.739085) is 0.739085.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.20</span> (hard, find the bug) The code is meant to find the weekly peaks, but it returns far too many.</p>
@@PYQ
import numpy as np
from scipy import signal
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
x = 10 * np.sin(2 * np.pi * np.arange(140) / 7) + rng.normal(0, 4, 140)
print(len(signal.find_peaks(x)[0]), len(signal.find_peaks(x, prominence=10, distance=5)[0]))
@@END
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> it prints {{LAST}} (the true count is 20, one per week). Plain <code>find_peaks</code> calls every tiny noise wiggle a peak. Constrain it: <code>prominence</code> says how much a peak must stand above its surroundings, and <code>distance</code> says how far apart peaks must be. Choose these from the domain (a weekly cycle means at least 5 days apart).</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.21</span> (hard, design) Delivery-time data has 30% exact zeros (pre-cooked items) and positive skew otherwise. How would you model it with SciPy?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a single lognormal cannot have exact zeros. Use a two-part (hurdle) model: estimate the zero share p0 as a proportion (with a <code>binomtest</code> interval), fit <code>lognorm.fit(positive_values, floc=0)</code> to the rest, and combine: P(T &gt; t) = (1 - p0) * lognorm.sf(t). Validate by simulating from the model and comparing quantiles with the data.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.22</span> (expert) Show that the standard error of the OLS slope in simple regression is sigma / sqrt(S<sub>xx</sub>), where S<sub>xx</sub> is the sum of squared deviations of x, and confirm numerically.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the slope is sum((x - mean x)(y - mean y)) / S<sub>xx</sub> = sum((x - mean x) y) / S<sub>xx</sub>, a linear combination of y with weights w = (x - mean x)/S<sub>xx</sub>. With independent noise of variance sigma<sup>2</sup>, the variance is sigma<sup>2</sup> * sum(w<sup>2</sup>) = sigma<sup>2</sup> * S<sub>xx</sub>/S<sub>xx</sub><sup>2</sup> = sigma<sup>2</sup>/S<sub>xx</sub>. Numerical confirmation against the matrix formula:</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(11)
x = rng.uniform(0, 10, 80)
y = 2 + 0.7 * x + rng.normal(0, 1.0, 80)
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(80), x])
beta = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)
s2 = ((y - X @ beta) ** 2).sum() / 78
se_matrix = np.sqrt(s2 * np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X)[1, 1])
se_formula = np.sqrt(s2 / ((x - x.mean()) ** 2).sum())
print(round(se_matrix, 6), round(se_formula, 6))
@@END
<p>Both routes give the same number, and the formula shows why a wider spread of x (larger S<sub>xx</sub>) gives a more precise slope: design your experiments to spread x out.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.23</span> (expert, interview) Why do numerical analysts say "never compute the inverse to solve a system", and what do you do when the same matrix must be solved against 1,000 right-hand sides?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> solving by LU costs about 2n<sup>3</sup>/3 operations and has backward-stable error behaviour; forming the inverse costs about 2n<sup>3</sup> and then a multiply, and its errors are larger when the matrix is ill-conditioned (condition number large). For many right-hand sides, factor once with <code>lu_factor</code> (or <code>cholesky</code> for symmetric positive-definite matrices), then call <code>lu_solve</code> for each (or pass all right-hand sides as columns of one matrix). Also check <code>np.linalg.cond</code>: if it is near 1/eps (about 10<sup>16</sup>), the answer has no correct digits whatever method you use.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.24</span> (expert, interview) Your KDTree nearest-neighbour search on 768-dimensional text embeddings is no faster than brute force. Why, and what are your options?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> in high dimensions distances to the nearest and farthest points become similar, so the tree cannot rule out whole boxes and must visit almost every node. Options: reduce dimension first (PCA or SVD, accepting some loss), use approximate nearest-neighbour libraries (graph or quantisation indexes), or batch brute force as a matrix multiplication on normalised vectors (<code>E @ q</code>), which is often fast enough up to a few hundred thousand vectors because it uses optimised BLAS.</p>

<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.P.25</span> (expert, design) An experiment has 10 million users per arm, and the data lives in a warehouse. How do you run the t-test without moving all rows?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> compute count, mean and variance per arm in SQL, then run the test from the summaries with <code>ttest_ind_from_stats</code>. It gives the same result as the row-level test:</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import stats

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
a = rng.normal(10.00, 3, 200000); b = rng.normal(10.02, 3, 200000)
full = stats.ttest_ind(b, a, equal_var=False)
summ = stats.ttest_ind_from_stats(b.mean(), b.std(ddof=1), b.size, a.mean(), a.std(ddof=1), a.size, equal_var=False)
print(round(full.pvalue, 8), round(summ.pvalue, 8), np.isclose(full.statistic, summ.statistic))
@@END
<p>The summaries must use the sample standard deviation (<code>ddof=1</code>). With ten million users per arm even tiny differences are significant, so judge practical size with the interval and the business threshold, and consider variance-reduction methods (pre-experiment covariates) before spending more traffic.</p>

<h2>Prompt pack</h2>
<div class="prompt"><div class="ph"><span>✨ AI prompt: copy and paste into any AI</span><button type="button" class="copy">Copy</button></div><pre>You are my patient, practical professor. I am a working professional moving into data roles, and English is my second language, so use simple English. Teach me SciPy and statsmodels step by step: scipy.stats distributions (pdf, cdf, ppf, rvs, fit), hypothesis tests (t-test, Mann-Whitney, chi-square, Shapiro), confidence intervals and the bootstrap, then optimize (minimize, curve_fit, linprog), integrate, interpolate and the OLS output table of statsmodels. Use small runnable Python examples with seeded randomness and show real output. After each part, ask me one check question and wait for my answer before continuing.</pre></div>
<div class="prompt"><div class="ph"><span>✨ AI prompt: copy and paste into any AI</span><button type="button" class="copy">Copy</button></div><pre>You are my patient, practical professor. I am a working professional learning scientific Python, and English is my second language. Review my analysis code below. Check: Is the statistical test right for my data (paired or independent, normal or skewed)? Do I report an effect size and a confidence interval, not only a p-value? Are random seeds set? Did I give curve_fit a starting guess and check the residuals? Did I use solve instead of inv, and sparse matrices where the data is mostly zeros? List the top 5 problems in order of importance, with a corrected snippet for each. My code: [PASTE HERE]</pre></div>
<div class="prompt"><div class="ph"><span>✨ AI prompt: copy and paste into any AI</span><button type="button" class="copy">Copy</button></div><pre>You are my patient, practical professor. I am a working professional preparing for data science interviews, and English is my second language. Run a 30-minute mock interview on SciPy and statsmodels: p-values, Welch versus Student, bootstrap versus formula intervals, power and sample size, curve_fit failures, optimisation methods, solve versus inv, sparse matrices, FFT aliasing and KDTree limits. Ask one question at a time, mixing concept questions with a small coding task. After each answer give a score out of 5, a model answer, and one tip on how to say it clearly in English.</pre></div>
